package videoproduct

// The two cinematic pipelines are separate because a 12-minute narrated piece
// and a 30-second
// social cut are different productions, not one production at two lengths:
// long-form needs chapters, retention management, character continuity across
// dozens of clips, and narration that the visuals are cut to. Short-form needs
// one arc landed in the first seconds and nothing else.
//
// Both can spend real money per generation call. That is why every stage
// here that can spend is gated on explicit authorization in its own human
// input, using the same rule the assets stage established.

// generationSpendGate is the authorization paragraph every paid stage carries.
// Kept in one place so the stages that spend cannot drift apart on what counts
// as approval; a test pins exactly which stages carry it.
//
// The rule is deliberately strict about WHERE approval comes from: this
// product bills the user per generation call, and an agent that reads
// enthusiasm about a storyboard as permission to spend is the failure mode
// worth designing against.
func generationSpendGate(what string) string {
	return "This stage can spend the user's money, so it has exactly two modes and you choose between them ONLY from the human input for THIS stage. " +
		"Treat generation as authorised solely when that input explicitly approves generating " + what + " or spending on it. " +
		"Approval of an earlier stage, enthusiasm about the plan, or silence is NOT authorisation.\n" +
		"MODE A — not explicitly authorised (the default): plan the work completely and produce nothing. Write the artifact this stage owes, with every intended call fully specified — resolved model id, provider, exact input, and the estimated number of paid calls — so the user can approve a concrete, costed plan rather than an idea. Finish by stating plainly what it will cost and what still needs approval.\n" +
		"MODE B — explicitly authorised: do the same planning, then actually make the calls, staying within any cost ceiling or call count this pipeline's brief recorded. Never record an asset as produced unless you created the file and confirmed it exists on disk and is non-empty; a call that fails is recorded as `failed` with its error, never described as if it succeeded. If you reach the approved ceiling before finishing, stop and report what remains rather than continuing to spend."
}

// falVideoJobLifecycleRule prevents a slow provider job from consuming an
// agent-shell timeout, then being submitted a second time after that timeout.
// It is deliberately a stage contract (rather than implementation advice): a
// video call can spend money and H3 reference jobs can outlive a short-lived
// execution tool by many minutes.
func falVideoJobLifecycleRule() string {
	return "For every paid Fal video call, use the non-blocking queue lifecycle: submit exactly once with the installed client's queue-submit API, then IMMEDIATELY persist a request record before waiting. The record must include the resolved endpoint, Fal request ID, exact non-secret input, submission timestamp, and intended output path. Do NOT run a foreground `fal.subscribe()` or other blocking generation command inside an execution tool that can time out. Poll/rejoin that same request ID with short bounded backoff (about 5 seconds initially, then 10–30 seconds), record provider status updates, and retrieve the result only after the provider reports a terminal success. If the agent, shell, or tool times out or reconnects, resume the persisted request ID; never submit the same generation again unless the user explicitly authorises a new paid attempt. Report provider submit, provider wait, download, and local validation durations separately so a slow provider is never confused with avoidable workflow delay. "
}

// A spending approval alone must not silently lock a recurring subject to an
// agent-selected model: that choice governs every later appearance.
func characterModelChoiceGate() string {
	return "Before creating any reference image, present at most three live-verified viable character-model choices. For each state the exact provider and model id, why its reference-image conditioning fits this subject, relevant output/control limits, and current billing evidence; recommend one, but NEVER silently select it. Ask the user to choose a named option (or name another model) in this stage's human input. A generic approval to make characters, an earlier provider preference, or a budget approval without a model choice is NOT a character-model selection. Do not make any paid character-reference call until that input contains both the selected provider/model and explicit approval to spend on the stated reference pack. Record the user's selected provider/model, pack, retry allowance, and cost evidence in the character artifact. "
}

var longformStageDescriptions = map[string]string{
	"longform-brief": "Own the brief before any production work. Read the request, the recent conversation, and every relevant file in uploads/. " +
		"This pipeline is for genuinely long-form video — roughly 8 minutes or more — so confirm the target duration explicitly rather than assuming; if the user actually wants something short, say so and stop rather than running a long-form production on a short brief. " +
		"Follow video-creation's request-understanding rules, and resolve the production-level unknowns video-model-selection lists before the first paid call, because every one of them is expensive to unwind later: provider or model preference, whether any character/presenter/product must recognisably recur across shots, the cost ceiling or maximum number of paid generation calls, and how many re-prompts a disappointing shot gets before you stop and ask. " +
		"Use longform-cinematic-video and video-storytelling to decide the narrative and cinematic contract at this length — which arc, which hook type, roughly how many chapters, visual language, camera grammar, color journey, sound world, and continuity priorities. " +
		"Write longform-brief.md with the message, audience, destination, target duration, aspect ratio, tone, narrative shape, cinematic contract, every resolved production decision above, the exact claims and their sources, assumptions, and blockers. Do not generate media and do not write the script yet.",

	"longform-script": "Turn longform-brief.md into longform-script.md, the piece's narrative spine. Follow video-storytelling: one arc across the whole piece, chapters of roughly 2-4 minutes each carrying their own smaller arc, beats connected with `but`/`therefore` rather than `and then`, and the hook and stakes fully landed inside the first 30 seconds. " +
		"Place the retention work deliberately: the first major payoff before 2:00, a re-hook every 3-4 minutes after the two-minute mark, an open loop planted early and paid off late, and pattern interrupts at the cadence the skill gives. Reserve the last ~20 seconds for the close and put no essential content there. " +
		"Write the narration verbatim — this exact text becomes the voiceover and drives every downstream duration, so it must be the words to be spoken, not a summary of them. Mark chapter and sequence boundaries, and for each beat state what the viewer should see, what is said, the dramatic turn it serves, and which claim or source it rests on. " +
		"Name every character, presenter, or product that recurs across more than one beat; the next stage defines them. Do not generate media.",

	"longform-characters": "Define every recurring character, presenter, animal, mascot, creature, product, vehicle, or other subject BEFORE any shot of it exists. This is the first paid-media decision in a cinematic production: the brief and script establish what the subject must do, then its reference must be approved before footage can be planned or made. This stage prevents the most common viewer-visible defect in AI-generated video — a face, outfit, product, or creature drifting between shots — and it cannot be done retroactively. " +
		"Read longform-brief.md and longform-script.md, then follow cinematic-visual-development and video-cinematography. Classify each recurring subject as human/presenter, animal/pet, mascot/creature, product/object, vehicle/robot, or environment; build the type-appropriate pack rather than treating every subject as a human face. For each subject produce a written spec at characters/<name>.md carrying 3-6 specific disambiguating visual attributes as one exact phrase to be reused verbatim in every later prompt, plus the selected approved reference-pack images under characters/<name>/. Keep them inside this step's own folder. " +
		"After the user chooses, commit one model and one provider per character's whole arc — record that choice, because later stages are bound by it and a mid-arc switch needs the user's explicit sign-off. Use video-model-selection to prepare the choices, and fal-ai or google-ai to generate; both skills show how to send and reuse a local reference image. " +
		"Offer budget, recommended, and premium packs before spending. The recommended human pack is front, three-quarter, profile, full-body, and sequence wardrobe; use cinematic-visual-development's appropriate recommended pack for non-human subjects. Write longform-characters.md listing every subject type, spec path, all reference paths, its user-selected committed model and provider, and any subject the brief mentions that deliberately does NOT recur. After generating a reference, call show_character for each recurring subject's primary reference and wait for explicit approval; show the additional pack images with show_reference. The shot list must not plan a recurring subject until its displayed reference pack is approved. If the brief needs no recurring subject, say so explicitly in longform-characters.md and generate nothing. " +
		characterModelChoiceGate() +
		generationSpendGate("character reference images"),

	"longform-look-sound": "Lock the production's visual and sonic world before narration or footage. Read longform-brief.md, longform-script.md, and longform-characters.md, then follow video-look-sound. Write longform-look-sound.md with visible sections for `Locations and backgrounds`, `Wardrobe, props, and continuity`, `Lighting and visual palette`, `Camera grammar`, `Speech and voices`, `Music`, `Ambience and sound effects`, and `Captions`. For every returning location, record its geography, time of day, weather, practical light sources, and the view that a later returning shot must match. For every recurring character, record wardrobe states and hero props by sequence. " +
		"When people or characters speak, present visible native lip-synced dialogue, consistent off-camera TTS voiceover, and hybrid routes with their performance, continuity, model, cost, and editing tradeoffs. Obtain an explicit choice; never allow an audio-incapable endpoint to silently choose the format. Do not generate media or spend money in this stage.",

	"longform-narration": "Generate the narration, because at this length the voiceover is the spine the visuals are cut to — deriving shot durations from an estimate instead of from measured audio is the most expensive mistake in this pipeline, forcing either regenerated clips or a rewritten script. " +
		"Read longform-script.md and follow video-editing's narration guidance. Generate the voiceover in per-beat or per-chapter segments, never as one long pass, so a single bad beat can be regenerated later without re-timing everything after it -- both providers' own guidance independently recommends chunking for the same reason. " +
		"Use whichever provider longform-brief.md committed to: `fal-ai` and `google-ai` both generate speech, so a production with only one provider's key configured is not blocked here. If the brief settled on a provider whose credential is missing, say so and stop rather than silently switching providers mid-production. " +
		"Then MEASURE what you actually got: run ffprobe on every segment and record its real duration. Do not record an estimate from the word count. " +
		"Write longform-narration.md listing each segment's beat id, chapter, script text, file path, and measured duration in seconds, plus the measured total. The next stage derives the entire shot plan from these numbers, so an unmeasured or missing segment is a blocker, not a rounding detail. " +
		generationSpendGate("narration or voiceover audio"),

	"longform-shotlist": "Turn the measured narration into the shot plan. Read longform-brief.md, longform-script.md, longform-characters.md, longform-look-sound.md, longform-narration.md, and multi-clip-cinematic-generation. If a recurring subject exists, proceed only when this step's human input explicitly approves its displayed reference; otherwise write a blocked shot list naming the approval still needed and do not prepare paid calls. " +
		"Derive every duration from longform-narration.md's MEASURED numbers — a chapter whose narration measures 94 seconds needs 94 seconds of visuals, and how many clips that is falls out of the chosen model's per-call duration, not the other way round. Do the arithmetic explicitly and show it. Plan chapters, sequences, and scenes before individual shots, and minimize independent calls and stitch points using the topology rules in longform-cinematic-video and video-provider-capabilities. Select a model per sequence from its live-verified continuity controls — longer or structured multi-shot generation, extension/reference-video continuation, start/end or multi-image conditioning, and native audio where needed — not merely from visual quality, familiarity, or price. If no endpoint supports the planned handoff, redesign that seam before spending. " +
		"For each shot record: its beat and chapter, its duration, what the shot must prove, the resolved model id and provider, and the full generation prompt built with video-cinematography's five-aspect formula — subject, subject motion, scene, spatial framing, camera — using precise camera vocabulary rather than mood adjectives. Any shot containing a recurring subject MUST use that subject's committed model and provider from longform-characters.md and repeat its spec phrase verbatim; where that model's audio support is wrong for the shot, plan it silent and add audio at assembly rather than switching models. " +
		"Classify every visual as generated cinematic footage, supplied media, or a HyperFrames insert. Use HyperFrames for approved illustrated or animated sequences, motion graphics, supplied-media compositions, UI/screens, typography, diagrams, overlays, and transitions. Preserve the requested style and continuity for each sequence. For every follow-up shot, choose its seam-generation route BEFORE it is made: one longer/multi-shot take, extension/reference-video continuation, last usable stable frame as the next start reference, a motivated camera-angle cut, or an intentional discontinuity. Where consecutive clips must flow together, overlap their prompts at the seam so clip N's last described moment matches clip N+1's first. Build the reference manifest and transition grammar required by multi-clip-cinematic-generation: aspect ratio/orientation, screen direction, eyeline, subject placement, lens/framing family, camera vector, action overlap, cut motivation, and stable character/location/prop references. Choose each generated shot's model with video-model-selection and confirm the model id against the provider's own live reference — never invent one. " +
		"Write longform-sequence-plan.json with every sequence's incoming and outgoing state, references, generation topology, exact follow-up seam-generation route, selected outgoing frame when applicable, camera handoff, boundary plan, transition grammar, reference manifest, seam risk, and the reason each independent generation is unavoidable. Write longform-shotlist.md with the total paid call count and estimated cost stated up front, since the next stage asks the user to approve exactly that. Generate nothing in this stage.",

	"longform-visual-development": "Turn the approved script, characters, look-and-sound direction, narration, and sequence plan into actual visual evidence before any video call. Read cinematic-visual-development, longform-shotlist.md, longform-sequence-plan.json, longform-characters.md, longform-look-sound.md, multi-clip-cinematic-generation, and video-provider-capabilities. A reference manifest that merely names a location is not evidence and may not be consumed by an anchor or follow-up clip. " +
		"Write longform-visual-development.md and longform-reference-manifest.json. For every recurring sequence, specify the approved location/background (including time of day and return geography), wardrobe and hero props, lighting/palette, camera family, and sound-world anchors. For every sequence with a continuity seam, create a reviewable start reference and a planned exit/end-state reference; include an extra bridge/reference frame whenever the selected endpoint accepts multiple references or an explicit end frame. Do not invent unsupported endpoint fields: record which selected endpoint controls are live-verified and which reference is only a human visual target. " +
		"When the human input explicitly authorises this reference-pack spend, generate the planned reference images under references/ and call show_reference for every image with its semantic role. Wait for explicit approval of the pack before footage uses it. If approval or a required image is absent, write a blocked artifact naming it. Record exact paths, roles, provider/model, prompts, estimated/actual cost, and the later sequence/shot each reference constrains. " +
		generationSpendGate("visual-development reference images"),

	"longform-anchor-shot": "Create exactly one approved anchor clip: the first clip of the named sequence from longform-shotlist.md and longform-sequence-plan.json. Read cinematic-visual-development, longform-visual-development.md, longform-reference-manifest.json, longform-characters.md, longform-narration.md, and multi-clip-cinematic-generation. Do not generate if its required reference images have not been shown and approved. Treat it as the opening of the planned multi-clip sequence, not a self-contained short: use the selected model's verified controls and deliberately preserve the approved exit state for its legal successor. The human input may name which planned sequence to begin; without a named sequence, choose the earliest unfinished sequence. Do not batch the remaining shots, and do not reinterpret this as permission to run a workflow. Use fal-ai, google-ai, or seeddance-api only with the recorded provider and model. For an entry explicitly classified as a HyperFrames insert, build and render only that planned insert with the managed HyperFrames tooling; do not introduce unplanned HyperFrames scenes. " +
		"Condition every recurring subject on its approved reference image from longform-characters.md, using the committed model, provider, and exact spec phrase. Respect the re-prompt budget in longform-brief.md; stop at the budget rather than spending indefinitely. Show the generated MP4 with show_video, inspect it, and reject an incorrect anchor before creating a successor. " +
		"Write longform-anchor-shot.md and longform-continuity-anchor.json. Record the planned and actual shot id, sequence, status (`generated`, `failed`, or `skipped`), exact local path, resolved provider/model, request prompt and seed if any, measured duration, usable trim range, selected last usable stable frame, reference manifest, aspect ratio/orientation, incoming/outgoing subject/prop/camera/screen-direction/eyeline/action/lighting/audio state, and the legal successor. A shot is not generated unless its non-empty file exists on disk. " +
		falVideoJobLifecycleRule() +
		generationSpendGate("the anchor video clip"),

	"longform-next-shot": "Create exactly one next clip, never a batch. Read cinematic-visual-development, longform-shotlist.md, longform-sequence-plan.json, longform-visual-development.md, longform-reference-manifest.json, longform-characters.md, longform-narration.md, longform-anchor-shot.md, longform-continuity-anchor.json, and multi-clip-cinematic-generation. Also read longform-next-shot.md and longform-continuity-ledger.json from this step folder when they already exist: they are the durable history from prior runs of this reusable recipe. The human input may name the target shot or give a precise creative adjustment. If it does not, select the next unfinished shot whose predecessor is accepted. If no accepted predecessor exists, do not invent a connection: write a blocked result naming the required anchor or predecessor. " +
		"Use the recorded seam-generation route instead of a fresh generic prompt: for every continuity successor call `minimax/h3-max/reference-to-video` with the accepted immediate predecessor in `reference_video_urls` as Video 1. Use the predecessor's selected last usable stable frame to define and inspect the handoff, not as a substitute route. Preserve the recorded reference manifest, orientation/aspect-ratio contract, camera handoff, screen direction, eyeline, and overlapping action. A planned camera-angle cut may change framing only when its cut motivation and scene state remain intact; define the new lens, camera vector, and matched entry action rather than describing an unrelated angle as a continuation. Condition recurring subjects on their existing approved reference, use the recorded provider/model, and respect the re-prompt budget in longform-brief.md. Do not silently create or replace a character reference. " +
		"Show the resulting MP4 with show_video and reject continuity contradictions before accepting it. Update longform-continuity-ledger.json cumulatively: retain the anchor and every prior accepted/rejected entry, then append this attempt with request lineage, exact local path, usable trim range, boundary frames, reference manifest, actual incoming/outgoing state, transition type, audio state, and legal successor. Write longform-next-shot.md identifying the attempted shot, its status (`generated`, `failed`, `skipped`, or `blocked`), and the next safe action. A shot recorded as generated without a non-empty file on disk is a failure. " +
		falVideoJobLifecycleRule() +
		generationSpendGate("the next video clip"),

	"longform-seam-proof": "Perform the lightweight receipt for the newest H3 Max successor. Read longform-continuity-anchor.json, longform-continuity-ledger.json, longform-next-shot.md, longform-reference-manifest.json, multi-clip-cinematic-generation, and video-stitching. Confirm the source MP4 with ffprobe and inspect its stable opening and ending frames; compare its opening to the predecessor ending only enough to catch an obvious break. Never render a seam-preview MP4, set trim points, call a generation provider, create a bridge clip, or use a dissolve/crossfade here. A visible continuity break is `fail`. " +
		"Write longform-seam-proof.md as a compact receipt naming the exact predecessor/successor paths, observed stable frames, and verdict (`pass`, `fail`, or `blocked`). A failed receipt prescribes one bounded replacement: regenerate the successor with `minimax/h3-max/reference-to-video`, using the accepted predecessor as Video 1 and an improved camera/action handoff. Do not create a third bridge clip. This receipt is informational; it does not block another accepted H3 continuation or final direct concat.",

	"longform-assemble": "Assemble, mix, and render the finished video from media that already exists. Read longform-shotlist.md, longform-sequence-plan.json, longform-continuity-anchor.json, longform-continuity-ledger.json, and longform-narration.md. Read video-stitching, video-editing, and longform-cinematic-video before working. Build longform-assembly-manifest.json directly from the accepted clip ledger: exact source version/path, timeline order, direct-cut boundary, and linked narration beat for every clip. Resolve every input from those exact recorded paths — do not write generation prompts, do not call any generation provider, and do not look for files elsewhere. If a required shot is `failed` or missing, stop and write longform-render.md naming it; never substitute an unrelated clip. HyperFrames may supply exact overlays, titles, or approved inserts, but must remain inside the same cinematic edit and sound world. " +
		"Follow video-editing throughout. Cut the visuals to the measured narration rather than the reverse. Verify every clip's specs with ffprobe before concatenating and normalise the minority that does not match, since one differently-specced clip breaks a stream-copy concat for the whole batch. Force constant frame rate where a source is variable, or audio drift accumulates audibly across a piece this long. " +
		"Use the plain direct concat in longform-cinematic-video. Do not add creative transitions, trim points, audio bridges, grading, or speed changes to repair a continuity miss; regenerate the H3 successor instead. Put exact text, titles, and captions in a deterministic overlay layer, never baked into generated footage. " +
		"Write longform-edit-decision-list.json with every source version, timeline time, direct cut, and linked narration beat. Render longform-final.mp4 in this step folder, verify it by full decode with ffprobe, and write longform-render.md with every input path, the commands used, and the measured duration, dimensions, frame rate, codecs, and loudness of the result.",

	"longform-check": qualityReviewDescription("longform-final.mp4 as named by longform-render.md", "longform-delivery.md") +
		" This is a long-form piece assembled from many independently-generated clips, so weight the review accordingly: produce one contact sheet per chapter rather than one unreadable sheet for the whole runtime, and cover every edit boundary across the set rather than sampling a few. " +
		"After video-quality's checks pass, run generated-video-quality's checks against the same candidate and add their results to the same quality-report.json: character_consistency (against every reference image in longform-characters.md, not from memory — drifting identity is this pipeline's characteristic defect and it is invisible unless compared directly), generation_artifacts, temporal_coherence at every stitch point, motion_plausibility, lip_sync where dialogue exists, clip_color_consistency, prompt_adherence against longform-shotlist.md, and narration_alignment against longform-narration.md's measured durations. Record evidence for joins in that single report; do not create a separate seam report. A candidate that fails any quality check is not ready to present. " +
		"Also verify the narrative promises longform-script.md made: the hook lands inside the first 30 seconds, each chapter pays off, the open loop planted early is actually resolved, and the ending delivers the message the brief asked for.",
}

